---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = "Fiverr vs Upwork Fees in India (2026) - Which Keeps More INR?";
const desc = "Fiverr 20% vs Upwork 10% + withdrawal cuts compared in INR with real examples. Which platform keeps more for Indian freelancers?";
---
<Layout title={title} desc={desc} crumbs={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Guides',href:'/#tools'},{label:'Fiverr vs Upwork Fees'}]}>
<main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 pb-10">
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border mt-4 p-6 sm:p-8">
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Affiliate disclosure: no affiliate links on this page. Pure comparison.</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">By Noyel G • Updated 7 Sept 2026 • Checked both fee pages this week</div>
<h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold mt-1">Fiverr vs Upwork fees: which keeps more INR?</h1>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1">Short answer: Upwork keeps more on big orders, Fiverr wins on small gigs you deliver fast. Numbers below.</p>

<p class="text-sm leading-7 mt-4">I started on Fiverr because “no bidding” sounded easy. First $50 order → $40 in hand after 20% cut. Same $50 on Upwork → $45 minus withdrawal. That ₹400+ gap per order is why I moved bigger projects to Upwork but kept small gigs on Fiverr. Here’s the exact math so you don’t learn it from your wallet like I did.</p>

<div class="mt-5 max-w-xl">
<div class="font-bold">Fee comparison, $100 order, $1 = ₹83</div>
<div class="border rounded-2xl mt-2 divide-y text-sm">
<div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 p-3 font-bold text-xs text-gray-500"><span></span><span>Fiverr</span><span>Upwork</span></div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 p-3"><span class="text-gray-600">Platform cut</span><b>20% → $80</b><b>10% → $90</b></div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 p-3"><span class="text-gray-600">Withdraw to India (~4%)</span><b>$76.80</b><b>$86.40</b></div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 p-3 bg-green-50"><span class="text-gray-600">In hand (₹)</span><b>~₹6,374</b><b>~₹7,171</b></div>
</div>
<p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Withdraw math: Payoneer/bank ~4% + conversion spread. Actual varies slightly.</p>
</div>

<div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-4 mt-4 text-sm leading-7 max-w-xl">
<span class="font-bold">When Fiverr still wins:</span> $10–25 gigs where buyers come to you (no proposals, no connects cost). Time saved finding work beats the 10% extra cut when you’re new with zero reviews.
</div>

<div class="mt-4 text-sm max-w-xl">
<div class="font-bold">My rule now</div>
<p class="text-gray-600 mt-1">Under $30, fixed-scope gig → Fiverr. Over $50 or hourly → Upwork. Run both numbers through <a href="/tools/upwork-fees-calculator" class="text-indigo-700 font-bold">Upwork calculator →</a> before quoting — a $40/hr Upwork rate is really ~$34.50/hr in hand.</p>
</div>

<div class="mt-4 text-sm bg-white border rounded-2xl p-4 max-w-xl">
<div class="font-bold">FAQ</div>
<p class="mt-1"><b>Upwork connects cost?</b> Yes, proposals cost connects (~₹15–20 each). Factor 5–10 connects per win into your first-month cost.</p>
<p class="mt-1"><b>Fiverr withdrawal?</b> Payoneer or bank, same ~4% style cut. 14-day clearance for new sellers — plan cash flow.</p>
<p class="mt-3">Tools: <a href="/tools/freelance-rate-calculator" class="text-indigo-700 font-bold">Rate calculator →</a> | <a href="/tools/editing-rate-calculator" class="text-indigo-700 font-bold">Editing rate →</a></p>
</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500 bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-3 mt-4 max-w-xl">How I compared: Upwork 10% marketplace fee + ~4% withdrawal vs Fiverr 20% + ~4%, $100 example at ₹83, Sept 2026. Fee pages change — verify before big quotes.</div>
</div>
</main>
</Layout>
